(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-vignettes"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a foreign body sensation after a day of metalwork. Fluorescein shows several fine vertical lines running down the cornea. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer for urgent surgical exploration","Evert the upper eyelid","Examine the lower fornix with a cotton bud","Order computed tomography of the orbit","Patch the eye and review in one week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vertical linear defects mean the object is under the upper lid, scraping with each blink. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which symptom indicates the macula has become involved in a retinal detachment?","choices":["The eye has become painful","The pupil has become irregular","Central visual acuity has dropped","Floaters have increased","Flashes have become more frequent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Field defects come first; central acuity falls with macular involvement. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 36-year-old has a corneal laceration with a cut lens capsule and an opaque, swollen lens. When is lensectomy usually performed?","choices":["Immediately, before the globe is closed","Later, once hyphema and inflammation settle","At the same time as the globe repair, always","Only if vision fails to improve after a year","Never, since the lens will clear on its own"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is required but often deferred from the globe repair. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 34-year-old machinist has photophobia, tearing and a foreign body sensation after a day at the lathe. Diffuse light shows nothing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order computed tomography of the orbit","Patch the eye and discharge","Prescribe topical anaesthetic for comfort","Slit lamp examination","Reassure and review in one week"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Larger objects show on diffuse light; smaller ones need the slit lamp. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 48-year-old has a deep laceration in the medial third of the lower lid. What is the consequence if it is not repaired properly?","choices":["Permanent ptosis","Loss of central vision","Corneal ulceration within days","Chronic photophobia","Chronic tearing"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The canalicular injury is what makes the medial third special. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A cycloplegic drop is prescribed for a patient with a hyphema. What is it doing?","choices":["Lowering the intraocular pressure directly","Preventing infection of the anterior chamber","Dissolving the clot","Constricting the pupil to trap the blood","Paralysing the ciliary body"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Atropine, homatropine and scopolamine are used this way. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 52-year-old is brought in after a road traffic collision with an obviously injured left eye, confusion and shallow breathing. Which is the most appropriate first action?","choices":["Call ophthalmology before anything else","Assess airway, breathing and circulation","Measure visual acuity in both eyes","Instil fluorescein and examine the cornea","Order computed tomography of the orbits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The eye is assessed after the patient is stabilised. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 55-year-old with a fresh retinal detachment asks how urgently she needs to be seen. Which is the most accurate response?","choices":["By an ophthalmologist within 24 hours","At the next available clinic within a month","Only if the curtain spreads further","Within a week, once the floaters settle","Immediately, but only if the other eye is affected too"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The referral is immediate. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 59-year-old describes flashes for two weeks, then a curtain yesterday. Which sequence explains this?","choices":["Choroidal neovascularisation with haemorrhage","Optic nerve inflammation","Arterial occlusion with retinal infarction","Vitreous detachment, then a retinal break","Retinal break first, then vitreous liquefaction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the usual order of events. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man was struck in the right eye by a baseball. The lid is swollen, the conjunctiva is deeply haemorrhagic and the globe feels soft. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Irrigate the eye with copious saline","Apply a firm pressure patch","Shield the eye and call ophthalmology","Measure the intraocular pressure","Instil a mydriatic and examine the fundus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A soft eye with massive haemorrhagic chemosis after blunt force is a globe rupture until proven otherwise. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 28-year-old has a hyphema and a distorted pupil after a penetrating injury. Which step must be omitted?","choices":["Measuring the pressure","Placing a rigid shield","Giving an antiemetic","Calling ophthalmology","Confirming tetanus status"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pressing on a possibly open globe risks extruding its contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 68-year-old on apixaban develops painful proptosis of one eye over an hour with no injury. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Thyroid eye disease","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Periorbital haematoma","Orbital cellulitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is not always traumatic, and anticoagulants are a listed cause. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A patient's optic nerve was damaged at the moment of an orbital injury. What should be expected from surgery?","choices":["Vision recovers spontaneously in most cases","The nerve regenerates over several months","It is unlikely to improve, and surgery may worsen it","Surgery reliably restores the lost vision","Surgery restores vision if done within 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is why the decision is made carefully. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 26-year-old was punched in the eye. He has periorbital swelling, numbness of the cheek, and double vision when looking upward. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Blowout fracture with inferior rectus entrapment","Medial wall fracture with medial rectus entrapment","Third nerve palsy","Traumatic optic neuropathy","Orbital cellulitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infraorbital numbness with upward gaze diplopia is the combination. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A conjunctival foreign body is visible on diffuse light examination in a 28-year-old. Which is the most appropriate removal method?","choices":["A sterile 27-gauge needle","A battery-operated burr","Copious irrigation alone","Referral for operative removal","A cotton-tipped applicator"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A conjunctival object lifts off with a swab. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has a confirmed globe rupture. Which is the definitive management?","choices":["Topical antibiotics and observation","Intravitreal antibiotics alone","Enucleation in every case","Immediate surgical repair","Pressure patching and review in 24 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Everything else is holding measures until then. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 47-year-old struck by a metal shard has an eye in which the pupil is drawn to one side toward a corneal wound and the anterior chamber looks flat. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Traumatic mydriasis","Corneal abrasion","Anterior uveitis","Acute angle closure","Open globe injury"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A pupil pulled toward the wound with a flat chamber is the picture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A conscious and cooperative 33-year-old has a periorbital laceration after an assault. Which examination elements are appropriate?","choices":["Gonioscopy of the drainage angle","Corneal sensation testing alone","Inspection, pupils, acuity and fields","Dilated fundoscopy first","Tonometry before anything else"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, done without causing further damage. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 44-year-old has clear rhinorrhoea after a fall. Which two bedside findings would support cerebrospinal fluid rather than nasal secretions?","choices":["A raised white cell count and fever","A positive dextrose stick and a halo sign","A negative dextrose stick and clear fluid","Purulent colour and a foul smell","Fluorescein uptake and photophobia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those are the two bedside tests described. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 30-year-old with a hyphema asks how long the dangerous period lasts. Which is the most accurate response?","choices":["There is no dangerous period once bleeding stops","The first month, evenly throughout","The first 72 hours, when most rebleeding happens","The first 24 hours only","The second week, once the clot retracts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A secondary haemorrhage is what costs vision permanently. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with sickle cell disease sustains a hyphema. Why does that history matter?","choices":["It rules out a rebleed","It makes aminocaproic acid unnecessary","It means the pressure need not be checked","It makes the hyphema resolve faster","It raises the risk of complications"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Sickle cell disease is specifically flagged. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which combination of findings should raise a basilar skull fracture after head trauma?","choices":["Ptosis, a dilated pupil and diplopia","A red eye with discharge and lid crusting","Diplopia on upward gaze with cheek numbness","Raccoon eyes, Battle sign and haemotympanum","Proptosis, chemosis and reduced acuity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those are the indirect signs. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 32-year-old has an orbital floor fracture with no entrapment, no globe injury and a clear maxillary sinus. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Ice and analgesia, with follow-up in 2 to 3 days","Antibiotics and admission","Urgent operative repair","Neurosurgical consult","Immediate ophthalmology consult for a true blowout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is the pathway when nothing else is involved. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 44-year-old has an orbital floor fracture with no entrapment and no globe injury, and blood is seen in the maxillary sinus. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Systemic corticosteroids","Ice, analgesia and antibiotics","Immediate operative repair","Ice and analgesia alone","Neurosurgical admission"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood in the sinus is what adds the antibiotic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 29-year-old has a wooden splinter driven into the periorbital tissue while gardening. Beyond removing nothing and imaging appropriately, which additional step is required?","choices":["Give a mydriatic to examine the retina","Arrange an urgent visual field test","Begin oral corticosteroids","Confirm tetanus is up to date","Start systemic antivirals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Penetration with metal or organic material carries the risk. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A patient with a suspected basilar skull fracture has clear fluid dripping from the nose that forms a double ring on the pillowcase. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Neurosurgery consult and admission","Discharge with outpatient follow-up","Nasal packing to stop the drainage","Ophthalmology consult","Prophylactic antibiotics alone, then discharge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A cerebrospinal fluid leak forces both. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 50-year-old has a laceration involving the tarsal plate of the upper lid. Which is the most appropriate action?","choices":["Apply tissue adhesive","Refer routinely in 2 to 3 weeks","Consult ophthalmology","Repair it with absorbable sutures in the department","Leave it open for 24 hours then close it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tarsal plate involvement is an explicit referral criterion. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 39-year-old fell from a ladder. She has bruising around both eyes, bruising behind one ear, and blood behind the eardrum. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bilateral orbital floor fractures","Nasal fracture with periorbital spread","Periorbital cellulitis","Bilateral periorbital haematomas from the fall","Basilar skull fracture"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Raccoon eyes, Battle sign and haemotympanum together. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 12-year-old scratched his cornea playing. His parent asks for anaesthetic drops for the pain at home. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Prescribe nothing at all","Decline, because they delay healing and can cause an ulcer","Prescribe them for 24 hours only","Prescribe them, since children tolerate pain poorly","Prescribe a corticosteroid drop instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This holds at any age. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman scratched her cornea removing a contact lens. Fluorescein confirms an abrasion. She asks for something for the pain to use at home. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Analgesia and a topical antibiotic","Topical anaesthetic drops four times daily","Topical anaesthetic drops for 48 hours only","Topical corticosteroid drops for comfort","No treatment, since abrasions heal regardless"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Take-home anaesthetic delays healing, masks worsening symptoms and can cause a corneal ulcer. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
